iterations.reply:
I didn't add the two operations requests. This node's `with` clause grants only `aap:caps/response-channel@0.1.0`. A log write and an HTTP fetch would need file and `http-client` authority this node doesn't have, and the metadata URL would also send the customer's message text to an external host. Those calls should go in a node whose grant covers them, or be handled by the host. The body below sends the reply and checks the `ensures` clauses.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{agent-response, delivery-confirmation};

    import aap:caps/response-channel@0.1.0;

    export run: func(reply: agent-response) -> delivery-confirmation;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::response_channel;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(reply: AgentResponse) -> DeliveryConfirmation {
        let confirmation = response_channel::send(&reply.text);

        // ensures delivered == true. The node cannot make a refused delivery
        // true, so a refusal traps instead of producing an output that
        // violates the contract.
        if !confirmation.delivered {
            panic!("response channel did not confirm delivery");
        }

        // ensures present(session_id). The session id comes from the host
        // channel and must not be empty.
        if confirmation.session.is_empty() {
            panic!("response channel returned no session id");
        }

        confirmation
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

